  <h1>How to learn German: a beginner plan</h1>
  <p class="lede">German looks tough because of its long words and grammar cases, but it is very logical — and it shares a lot of vocabulary with English. Learn the sounds, the articles and the basic sentence pattern first, and German starts to make sense surprisingly quickly.</p>

  <div class="ad-slot" role="complementary" aria-label="Advertisement"><span>Advertisement</span></div>

  <h2>Step 1 — Learn German sounds</h2>
  <p>German spelling is much more regular than English spelling. Learn the special letters early: ä, ö, ü, and ß (which sounds like “ss”). Learn the “ch” sound and the difference between “w” (sounds like English “v”) and “v” (sounds like English “f”).</p>
  <p>Read German words aloud from the start. Because the spelling is regular, reading aloud trains your pronunciation and your ear at the same time.</p>
  <h2>Step 2 — Learn der, die, das with every noun</h2>
  <p>Every German noun has a gender: der (masculine), die (feminine) or das (neuter). Always learn the article with the noun — der Hund, die Katze, das Haus — never the noun alone. There are patterns that help (most words ending in -ung are feminine, most ending in -chen are neuter), but learning the article with the word is the reliable way.</p>
  <p>Start with useful words: greetings (Guten Tag, danke, bitte), numbers, time, food, family and everyday verbs like sein (to be), haben (to have) and gehen (to go).</p>
  <h2>Step 3 — Learn the four cases, one at a time</h2>
  <p>German nouns change their article depending on their job in the sentence — this is the case system. Start with just two: the nominative (the subject — der Mann) and the accusative (the object — den Mann). Learn the accusative with the verb: “Ich sehe den Mann” (I see the man).</p>
  <p>Add the dative and genitive later, one at a time. Many learners try to learn all four cases at once and get stuck. One case at a time, with real sentences, is much faster in the end.</p>
  <h2>Step 4 — Learn German word order</h2>
  <p>In German, the verb usually comes second in a statement: “Ich lerne Deutsch” (I learn German). In a question, the verb comes first: “Lernst du Deutsch?” And after words like “weil” (because), the verb goes to the end. These rules feel strange at first, but they are consistent — and once they click, German sentences become predictable.</p>
  <p>Practise by building sentences with the same pattern and swapping the words: change the subject, the object and the time, and you have ten new sentences from one pattern.</p>
  <h2>Step 5 — Listen and speak every day</h2>
  <p>Listen to German daily — slow news for learners, music, podcasts or shows with German subtitles. German is spoken clearly, which makes it good for listening practice. Read short texts and notice how the cases and word order work in real sentences.</p>
  <p>Speak from early on. A tutor helps especially with German, because they can correct your articles and word order — the two things that are hardest to learn from a book alone.</p>
  <h2>Common mistakes to avoid</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Trying to learn all four cases at once before you can make a simple sentence.</li>
    <li>Learning nouns without der, die or das, then guessing every time.</li>
    <li>Being scared of long words — they are just smaller words joined together.</li>
    <li>Translating English word order directly into German.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Practical tips</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Learn every noun with its article — der, die or das — from day one.</li>
    <li>Learn one case at a time, with real sentences.</li>
    <li>Read aloud daily; German spelling rewards it.</li>
    <li>Break long words into parts: “Bahnhof” is Bahn (track) + Hof (yard).</li>
  </ul>

