  <h2>Questions people ask</h2>

  <details><summary>How long does it take to learn German?</summary><p>With 30 minutes a day, most learners can handle simple everyday conversations in 6–12 months. German grammar takes time, but the vocabulary overlap with English and the regular spelling help a lot. Reaching a comfortable level for work or study usually takes 2–3 years of steady learning.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Is German hard to learn?</summary><p>German has hard parts — cases, gender and word order. But it has easy parts too: regular spelling, familiar vocabulary, and very consistent rules. Most learners find the beginning slow and the middle much smoother, once the patterns click.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Do I need to learn all the cases?</summary><p>Eventually, yes — but not at the start. Learn nominative and accusative first, then dative, then genitive. You can say a great deal with just the first two, and you will understand German speakers even if your cases are not perfect yet.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Can I learn German by myself?</summary><p>Yes, for vocabulary, grammar and reading. Speaking and getting corrections on articles and word order need a real person — that is where a tutor helps most.</p></details>
